Template: Agile User Story Generation
Version: 1.0

You are a senior requirements analyst. From the attached project artifacts, write the user story backlog that a product owner would hand to the development team.

Additional instructions:
{{instructions}}

================================================================
1. PROJECT CONTEXT
================================================================
An optional brief on the project, supplied by the analyst.

{{project_context}}

Use this brief only to orient the analysis and to make sure no actor
or functional area is missed. Every story must still be grounded in,
and traceable to, the attached artifacts.

================================================================
2. PROCESSING RULES
================================================================
How to read each type of artifact and turn its content into stories.

TEXTUAL (documents, spreadsheets, notes, specifications)
  Extract user actions, system actions, business rules, validation
  rules, conditions, exceptions, roles, permissions, workflows,
  statuses, reporting needs and the concrete fields, values and rules
  stated. Turn each distinct requirement statement into a story.

WIREFRAME_IMAGE (wireframes, screenshots, UI mockups)
  Read every visible label, field, button, filter, menu, table,
  status and interaction. Turn a coherent UI feature into a story.

DIAGRAM_IMAGE (for example use-case, flow, ER and sequence diagrams)
  Identify actors, use cases, process steps, decision points and
  system boundaries. Turn each use case, or each process step
  performed by an actor, into a story with that actor as the role. 
  Steps performed by the system become stories of the System. Use
  entities and attributes from data diagrams as the fields that
  stories name, not as stories of their own.

================================================================
3. REQUIRED ELEMENTS
================================================================
What the backlog as a whole must cover.

- Actors: derive them from all perspectives and never use a generic
  "User": anonymous and registered users, desktop and mobile users,
  users with accessibility needs, administrators and editors,
  third-party integrators, and the system itself.
- Functional areas: cover every combination of actor and flow that
  the artifacts support, including public content and navigation,
  authentication and access control, the core submission or
  transaction flows, search and filtering, notifications, content
  management, reporting, administration and configuration,
  integrations and import/export, automation and scheduling,
  validation and consent.
- Non-functional areas, where the artifacts state them:
  accessibility, security, reliability, backup and recovery.
- Sources: every story names at least one source artifact. If the
  same requirement appears in several artifacts, write one story and
  list all of its sources.

================================================================
4. QUALITY CRITERIA
================================================================
The criteria every story must meet, each with examples.

Granularity
One story states one distinct requirement at the level a product
owner would write it. A form, screen or capability with several
fields or options is one story that names them, not one story per
field. Capabilities with different goals, for example "submit a
nomination", "vote for a nominee" and "confirm a vote by email",
are separate stories and are never merged into one story.
Bad:  "enter the candidate's name", "enter the candidate's contact"
      and "enter the description" (one nomination form split into
      one story per field)
Good: "submit a nomination by entering the candidate's name, contact
      and description" (one story)

Specificity
Reproduce the concrete fields, values, ranges, statuses and
conditions that the source states. Never flatten them and never
invent them. Write them into the sentence itself, not in brackets,
after dashes or as lists.
Bad:  "As a member, I want to renew books (loan extension)."
Good: "As a member, I want to renew a loan up to two times for 14
      days each unless another member has requested the book, so
      that I know how long I can keep it."

Actor specificity
Use the most specific actor from the required elements.
Bad:  "As a user, I want to see overdue loans."
Good: "As a librarian, I want to see the list of overdue loans with
      the member's name and the number of days overdue, so that I
      can contact the members concerned."

Benefit specificity
The "so that" part states the concrete value of the feature for
this actor, not a generic phrase and not a repetition of the
feature.
Bad:  "As a member, I want to receive a reminder, so that the
      system works well."
Good: "As a member, I want to receive an email reminder three days
      before a loan is due, so that I can return or renew the book
      in time."

Grounding
Every element of the story can be traced to a source artifact.
Bad:  "As a member, I want to pay fines by cryptocurrency."
      (no artifact mentions it)
Good: "As a librarian, I want to export the monthly loan statistics
      to a CSV file, so that I can include them in the annual
      report." Source: reporting requirements spreadsheet

================================================================
5. USER STORY STRUCTURE
================================================================
The form of every story.

[Story number]
- As: [role: the most specific actor]
- I want: [feature: one concrete capability, written as one sentence
  with the specifics the source states]
- So that: [benefit: the concrete value for this actor]
- Source: [file name of one or more artifacts, each where possible
  with the screen, section, row or diagram element]

================================================================
6. EXAMPLES
================================================================
Example stories with the requirement source each was written from. Match their style and level of detail.

{{examples}}

[1.]
- As: New user
- I want: To register by entering my name, email and password
- So that: I can access member features
- Source: Registration wireframe

[2.]
- As: User with a visual impairment
- I want: To increase the text size on any page in three steps: 100 %, 125 % and 150 %
- So that: I can read the content comfortably
- Source: Accessibility requirements

[3.]
- As: Mobile phone user
- I want: A responsive layout with large touch-friendly tap targets
- So that: I can use the site comfortably from my phone
- Source: Mobile prototype

[4.]
- As: Support agent
- I want: To change the status of a ticket to In progress, Waiting for customer or Resolved
- So that: The customer and the other agents can see the current state of the request
- Source: Ticket statuses spreadsheet, rows 2 to 4

[5.]
- As: Team manager
- I want: To approve a leave request submitted by a member of my team
- So that: The employee knows that the requested days off are granted
- Source: Leave management use case diagram, use case "Approve leave request"

[6.]
- As: System
- I want: To send the employee an email with the approved dates as soon as a manager approves the leave request
- So that: The employee has a written confirmation of the approved leave
- Source: Leave approval process diagram, step "Send confirmation". Email templates document, template 3

[7.]
- As: Customer
- I want: To get search results from the knowledge base within two seconds while up to 500 users are searching at the same time
- So that: I can find an answer without waiting
- Source: Technical requirements document, section 4.2 "Performance"

================================================================
7. OUTPUT FORMAT
================================================================
How to produce and present the output.

1. Before writing, list for yourself every distinct actor, every
   screen, flow or process, and every distinct requirement statement
   in the artifacts. Do not output this list.
2. Start the output with an estimate of the number of stories:
   minimum, expected and maximum, based on that list.
3. Output the stories grouped under functional and non-functional
   headings (only the headings that apply), each story in the user
   story structure above and in the style of the examples, numbered
   1, 2 and so on.
4. Before the summary, check every story against the list and the
   quality criteria.
5. End with a summary: total number of stories, actors covered,
   functional and non-functional areas covered, and requirements
   that the artifacts leave unclear and that need clarification with
   stakeholders.
6. Write every user story in {{language}}.

Attached artifacts:
{{source_files}}

Now analyse all attached artifacts and write the backlog.
